# Tutorial 27 — Editor Workflows
# Reference: docs/editor/core/editor.md
# Read scripts/tutorial/README.md for the complete course and linked references.
@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_27
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The editor connects text, visual tools, diagnostics, assets, and the running game. Use each view to answer a concrete authoring question.
@label lesson_menu
> Navigate a project and its story -> navigation
> Inspect and fix a scene -> diagnostics
> Use visual authoring tools -> tools
> Practice a complete edit cycle -> practice
> Checkpoint -> checkpoint
> Finish and return to the hub -> return_hub
@label navigation
Lavender: Project Explorer locates scripts, configuration, and assets. The text editor owns the source. Story Timeline shows arcs across files; Label Flow Map shows branches inside a script.
Lavender: Open the generated story map and find this workshop's route from the hub. Follow its script path, entry label, and return route.
Lavender: Asset Browser, image attributes, and the Inspector reveal what a selected resource or scene element actually contains. Clear names make all these views more useful.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label diagnostics
Lavender: VNS Diagnostics catches authoring problems before you play. Use the Run Console for runtime logs, errors, and the command that launched the project.
Lavender: Exercise: deliberately misspell a label in a copied script, find the diagnostic, repair it, and run the corrected path. Then try a missing asset path and compare the evidence.
Lavender: A clean parse does not prove every branch is reachable or every asset looks right. Combine static diagnostics, flow inspection, and playtesting.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label tools
Lavender: Puppeteer edits motion and exports timeline source. Layout Launcher opens dialogue and menu design. Layered Image Visualizer checks character compositions. Scene Lighting Studio prepares lighting presets.
Lavender: Image Tint and image attributes help prepare resources. Plugin Tools hosts extensions such as Pikupiku. Version Control helps review what changed before you share it.
Lavender: Treat generated source as part of your project. Inspect the export, keep meaningful names, and verify that re-opening the tool preserves the intended setup.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label practice
Lavender: Change one expression, one line, and one choice in a copy of this lesson. Preview the result, run the full game, inspect the diff, and commit the three intentional changes together.
Lavender: Use Git branches for experiments and keep build output out of source control. A review should make it clear which change affects story, assets, layout, or code.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label checkpoint
Lavender: Which view answers whether a cross-file story route points at the intended arc?
> Story Timeline and its story-map source -> correct
> Only the image tint controls -> retry
@label retry
Lavender: Tint edits appearance. Story routes live in the story map and the scripts it references.
[jump checkpoint]
@label correct
Lavender: Yes. Choose a tool based on the question you need answered.
[jump return_hub]

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_27]
[flag tutorial_done_27]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_6]
